<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Egypt</h1><p class="st-meta">1922 CE – 1953 CE · capital Cairo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Egypt's constitutional monarchy under Fuad I and Farouk, independent in name but under British influence, overthrown by the Free Officers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain's</a> declaration of February 1922 ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-egypt">Sultanate of Egypt</a>, recognising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as independent while reserving defence, communications, foreign interests and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuad-i-of-egypt">Fuad I</a> took the title of king, and a constitution followed in 1923.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Parliamentary politics were dominated by the struggle between the popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wafd-party">Wafd</a> party, the palace and the British. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-egyptian-treaty-of-1936">Anglo-Egyptian Treaty of 1936</a> kept British troops in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/panama-canal-zone">Canal Zone</a>. In 1942 British tanks surrounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdeen-palace">Abdeen Palace</a> to force <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farouk-of-egypt">Farouk</a> to appoint a Wafd government, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> advance was halted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-alamein">El Alamein</a>. Defeat in the 1948 war against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Israel</a> discredited the regime.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> riots of January 1952, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-officers-movement-egypt">Free Officers</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-naguib">Muhammad Naguib</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> seized power in July. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farouk-of-egypt">Farouk</a> abdicated for his infant son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuad-ii-of-egypt">Fuad II</a>, and the monarchy was abolished in June 1953, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Egypt" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Mysid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Egypt_(1922-1958).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1922 CE – 1953 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cairo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fuad I, Farouk, Fuad II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam; Coptic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Egypt</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Egyptian Kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
